<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · date unknown · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Doraji taryeong</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ko">도라지타령</bdi> / <bdi lang="ko-Latn">Doraji taryeong</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Korean folk song named after the doraji, the balloon flower, loved in both North and South Korea.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Koreans, in North and South Korea and in China</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Eunyul, Hwanghae, Korea</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doraji-taryeong">Doraji taryeong</a>&quot; is a Korean folk song that began in Eunyul, in Hwanghae province. The version sung today is counted among the folk songs of Gyeonggi, because its rhythm and melody changed over time and took on the character of that region's songs.</p><p>Its title names the doraji, the Korean name for the plant Platycodon grandiflorus, the balloon flower, and for its root. The song is set to the fast rhythmic pattern (jangdan) called semachi, at times switching to the jungmori pattern, and like other traditional Korean songs it uses a five-note scale.</p><p>It is one of the most popular folk songs in both North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> and among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and it is well known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under the name Toraji.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A light folk song built around the doraji, the balloon flower and its root. The mood is lively, carried by its fast dance-like rhythm.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Korean</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Korean</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">도라지 도라지 도라지</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Doraji doraji doraji</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Doraji, doraji, doraji,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">심심산천의 백도라지</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">simsimsancheonui baekdoraji</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>white doraji of the deep, deep mountains and streams:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">한두 뿌리만 캐어도</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">handu ppuriman kaeeodo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>though I dig up only one or two roots,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">대바구니로 반실만 되누나</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">daebaguniro bansilman doenuna</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they fill a bamboo basket half full [?].</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">에헤요 에헤요 에헤애야</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Eheyo eheyo ehaeya</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Eheyo, eheyo, ehaeya,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">어여라난다 지화자 좋다</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">eoyeorananda jihwaja jota</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>eoyeorananda, jihwaja, how fine!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">저기 저 산 밑에 도라지가 한들한들</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">jeogi jeo san mite dorajiga handeulhandeul</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Down there at the foot of that mountain the doraji sway and sway.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">도라지 도라지 도라지</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Doraji doraji doraji</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Doraji, doraji, doraji,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">은율 금산포 백도라지</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Eunyul Geumsanpo baekdoraji</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>white doraji of Geumsanpo in Eunyul:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">한 뿌리 두 뿌리 받으니</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">han ppuri du ppuri badeuni</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>one root, two roots I take up [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">산골에 도라지 풍년일세</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">sangore doraji pungnyeonilse</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it is a rich year for doraji in the mountain valley.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">도라지 도라지 도라지</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Doraji doraji doraji</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Doraji, doraji, doraji,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">강원도 금강산 백도라지</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">Gangwon-do Geumgangsan baekdoraji</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>white doraji of Geumgangsan in Gangwon-do:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">도라지 캐는 아가씨들</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">doraji kaeneun agassideul</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the young women digging doraji,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ko">손맵시도 멋들어졌네</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ko-Latn">sonmaepsido meotdeureojyeonne</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>how fine is the skill of their hands!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; the Korean text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doraji_taryeong" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Revised Romanization of Korean).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>doraji is the balloon flower (Platycodon grandiflorus), whose root is dug and eaten; kept as the song's word throughout.</li><li>line 1.4: 'bansil' is not standard Korean; read here as 'half full' (ban, half). The sense, a basket filled by only one or two roots, is a picture of plenty.</li><li>lines 2.1 and 2.2: 'eheyo', 'ehaeya' and 'eoyeorananda' are sung syllables with no fixed meaning, kept as syllables; 'jihwaja' is a cheer of delight.</li><li>line 3.2: Eunyul is a county in Hwanghae Province; Geumsanpo a place there.</li><li>line 3.3: 'badeuni', literally 'as (I) receive'; the sense here may be 'as I gather them'.</li><li>line 4.2: Geumgangsan, the Diamond Mountains, in Gangwon Province.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
